well you know I've spoken all over the  place and I'm a storyteller but the  problem is that I can go on for hours  and hours and hours and my you know with  just one story  so actually I'm going to use these  visuals so that I can get everything in  that I want to in the time that I have I  hope that's okay with you if it's not  it's okay  anyway I'm gonna do it when I was 15  years old I wrote this poem about the  school I was going to I hate this darn  unthinking school which professes to  teach you the golden rule you fool me  and I'll make you a fool against this I  will repel many's the time when I've  hated to stay when the board boring  teacher had nothing to say but no says  the teacher you can't go away and this I  will also retell so I learned what the  board boring teacher had taught and  thusly I learned to be bored on the spot  and ever since I've been bored at the  thought of the trash that the school has  to sell oh I'm sure education in schools  not all bad and I'll know things of  interest when I am a grad but the  camouflaged job on the interest is sad  and the learning won't set very well and  so every morning at just 8 o'clock I  rush into the school and behind me they  lock the door to my prison and I start  to walk through the prison from cell to  cell  so you can see I already had a bad  attitude when I was 15  oh it had about it I think I know what  that attitude came from when I was very  young our family would visit my mother's  parents in Boston every week or two my  grandfather William Blatt was a lawyer  and occasional judge he had been  president of the Massachusetts law  Society but his real passion was writing  and philosophy when we visited him my  grandfather would sit down with me and  asked me what I wanted to learn that  simple act was the most powerful  influence of my idea of how learning  should take place people probably  thought he was crazy talking to a  seven-year-old about atheism agnosticism  about what makes things humorous the ego  the iddah and the super-ego the causes  of the world wars Einstein's theory  great discoveries in science anything I  wanted to know but I remember these  things to this day it seemed perfectly  sensible to ask me what I was interested  in what I wanted to learn my grandfather  also wrote epigrams little sayings that  appeared every day over the headlines of  the Boston Post in fact to honor him we  recently published a book of his  epigrams with cartoons made by Albert  lamb here are a couple of them one we  get so used to slavery that we regard  our manacles as ornaments two if you  always do as you're told you will always  be told what to do and they're still  relevant after 60 years not long after  that I wrote after I wrote that poem I  organized some of my friends into a  group we called the thinkers club very  modestly we met at each other's houses  there were all the elite students of the  school except for me they went to  Harvard Yale and Princeton we got  together about twice a week and our  agenda was to brainstorm any questions  we wanted to discuss without censorship  there were no adults involved if we  didn't know the answers we went out and  found deaf speakers or went to see  rabbis ministers and philosophy students  I now realize that that was the first  school that I started  a comedian on Saturday Night Live told  the story about a university dean who  said you know at our college we teach  one year of language in one day we teach  one year of language in one day when  asked how they could do that he said  well you know when you take a language  in school and a year later you only  remember four words we teach those four  words of course we laugh because we  assume that people don't remember what  they learned in school we also assume  that children hate school but why do we  accept this modern brain research shows  very clearly that children are natural  learners the brain wants to learn I  think people's negative experiences  carry over to their jobs for the rest of  their lives  I think people accept jobs they hate  because they're used to schools they  hate in my experience graduates have  learner-centered and democratic schools  usually find jobs they like to do right  if your child says he or she hates  school listen to that that means  something's wrong with their school and  you need to get your child out of it and  find an alternative an alternative  school is one which is based on the  interest of the learner and empowers  them to make decisions about their own  education we were involved with learner  centered up centered approaches believe  that children are natural learners in  fact when you force children to learn  things that they aren't interested in it  tends to extinguish that natural ability  to learn the human spirit is very strong  so students may resist that  extinguishing for many years but after  five or six years the first paradigm  becomes self-fulfilling and kids appear  to become lazy and not interested in  learning for its own sake that interest  can sometimes be rekindled in the right  environment but in democratic schools we  often observe a period of detoxification  that takes place and students  coming from traditional schools  detoxification is a period of expression  of anger and having been forced to learn  things they weren't interested in  generally the longer they were in a  coercive environment the longer it takes  to recover the natural ability to learn  but after that that can be an explosion  of self-motivated learning how fast can  a student learn a lesson been forced to  learn we once had an intern who had been  unschooled he was always able to follow  his own interest at sixteen he decided  to go to college but in order to get  into the college he chose one of the  tests he had to pass was algebra and  he'd never been interested in algebra  had never studied it so he decided to  study algebra on his own for one week  then aced the test he even taught others  so now we have a ratio what is supposed  to take a year took a week a 52 to 1  ratio for a non schooler nice ratio huh  so if students are able to have the  freedom to follow their own interests  what happens when you have a whole group  or a whole school of them this is where  democratic process comes in the  democratic meeting ensures that the  freedom and rights of the individual do  not encroach upon the rights of others  in the group and it goes beyond that the  interactive process usually comes up  with better solutions than any one  individual could it is a  learner-centered process for a group the  word democracy is one of the most  overused words these days which tends to  water down its meaning but democracy in  this case means something very specific  to people in democratic schools the  empowerment of a group of people to make  real decisions in their meetings and  whatever it is decided in that meeting  actually happens I think that the best  way to prepare students to participate  in a democratic society is to have them  grow up in democratic school  why do we why do we have them experience  12 years of a dictatorship and then  expect them to be ready for democracy  we've developed a form of democratic  process that we learned from the mohawks  of the Iroquois Confederacy the Iroquois  Confederacy was the model that Jefferson  and Franklin used when they created the  plan for democratic decision-making for  the United colonies of North America  well that's what they called our  respective country at first I actually  saw a Franklin's letter in the archives  in Washington at that time the only  other models around were kings and  queens monarchy back in the late 1960s I  visited the Mohawk tribe in upstate New  York and learned how the Iroquois made  decisions for their Confederacy and  their approach the minority was honored  and allowed to say why they voted  against the majority if they wished they  or others could then ask for more  discussion with new and hopefully better  proposals if nobody asked for a revote  the community was saying that the final  proposal was acceptable we developed a  process of on that approach which I've  demonstrated in many places around the  country and around the world when I go  into a democratic meeting I'm fully  prepared to advocate from my point of  view but I'm also confident that the  power of the meeting the combining of  all those free wills will come up with  something better something we all never  thought of it takes a little longer than  pure democracy but the decisions seem to  be made more effectively and with more  authorities I sometimes heard of this  approach called unstructured but a true  democracy has a very complex structure  sometimes I compare it to Penn Station  at rush hour it looks like chaos to  those who don't understand it but  actually every single person has a place  to start from and the place they expect  to get to in the democratic school  virtually everyone knows about all that  decisions that have been made now that's  pretty structured and disciplined I  think the power of the democratic  meeting is something to behold let me  give you an example of a demonstration I  did for a small school in Virginia in  the meeting the girls complained that  they wanted a girls room rather than a  unisex bathroom that was voted on and  passed but one person voted against it  when asked why he pointed out that the  school would be moving in three weeks so  be a waste of money and could be applied  to the new building of course in the  revote it wasn't passed when this  happens in my demonstrations it is very  important because people realize that  decisions are not for ordained or made  in stone with democratic process the  participants learn how to listen to  every voice when we were organizing  Brooklyn free school we would have  meetings in people's apartments every  two weeks we encouraged people to bring  their children but they would run off  the kids would play amongst themselves  at one meeting people have brought three  six-year-olds I told the group that I  was sure these children had opinions  about what the proposed school should be  like so they were asked to come into the  meeting when they realized that we knew  that they had opinions one six-year-old  said it's very important that the school  not be too clean or have no furniture if  it did we wouldn't be able to run around  and jump and have fun people would worry  too much about the furniture of course  it was right but who else but a  six-year-old would have thought of this  I learned something new every time I'm  asked to demonstrate democratic process  I'll give you a few examples in India I  was asked to help demonstrate who asked  to help democratize a school with 2,500  students in it  nobody in the United States has ever  asked me to do that eventually we  established a system in which each class  would ever representative lead a meeting  in their classroom and votes on the  issues on the agenda would be taken and  votes tallied those representatives  would bring the votes with them to be  told in the Parliament so it wasn't a  representative system in this usual  sense and all the students could have  the experience of speaking in a  relatively small group situation not  lying along after that I was asked to  demonstrate democratic process in a  school we had helped to start in our  school start of course the oldest  student was five years old even though  I've done this many times I'm always  shocked at the power of the results  because I did not grow up in such a  system so as I drove over I had serious  doubts about whether this would work I  was thinking that at least I would have  to make the agenda for them and as I  walked in i heard a two-year-old  screaming mom oh I was thinking well  when they gathered around the table I  told them that at a meeting we could  talk about good ideas for the school or  problems in the school immediately most  of the hands went up we had our agenda  about such subjects as not going outside  if you had a cold and it was cold out  and not eating chocolate in the  afternoon because it had a caffeine like  substance in it that was brought up by a  four year old my organization the  alternative education resource  organization has helped start more than  50 new educational alternatives in the  last few years probably the biggest  revolution that could take place in  schools all across the country would be  to simply have the classroom door open  and have a worthwhile place for the kids  to go if they don't want to be in the  class this is already the case in the  school of self-determination an  inner-city public school with 600  students  in this school the students have a  constitutional right to leave any class  they want without explanation the  decisions in the school are made by a  democratic Parliament the kids also  interview the teachers before they're  hired the teachers get to do a practice  class and the students vote on which  teachers are going to be hired oh and  where's this school in inner-city Moscow  Russia  my mission is from is to promote the  education revolution to make  learner-centered empowering education  available to students everywhere I will  help anyone who wants to change the  system from within but personally I  think we're more likely to change it by  creating good models outside the system  democratic process is not hard to do let  me show you what I mean who here in the  audience has some ideas or proposals on  how to make education really learner  centered and empowering to students who  has some ideas okay let's have you just  come up to the stage just for a minute  quickly quickly people who have ideas  come on up Oh guys make a circle make a  circle  okay  you're late oh this is how it starts  thank you  you
 

